<section class="page-head">
<div>
<div class="eyebrow">Path Two · Modernism Listening</div>
<h1>Modernist Concert Music</h1>
<p class="lede">Hear repetition become a ground, a pulse come under pressure, relationships create coherence without a tonal home, and color become part of musical form.</p>
</div>
<span class="page-sig" aria-hidden="true"><PeriodMotif unit="05-modernism" role={5} /></span>
</section>

<p class="threshold">Modernist composers reorganize expectation from the inside—repetition can hold a passage together, accents can press against the pulse, relationships can create coherence without a governing tonal home, and instrumental color can shape musical motion and form.</p>
<section class="intro-ways"><h2>Four ways to build with sound</h2><p>Music can be organized by what repeats, where stress falls, how pitches relate, and which instruments carry the sound. In Modernist works, these elements help create continuity, tension, direction, and form.</p></section>

<div class="section-terminator" aria-hidden="true"><PeriodMotif unit="05-modernism" role={3} /></div>

<div class="loading-note" id="loading-note"><span class="spin" aria-hidden="true"></span>Preparing listening examples…</div>

