<div><span class="lens-idx">02 · Collision</span><h2 class="lens-title">Accent against pulse</h2></div>
</div>
<p class="lens-idea"><strong>The musical idea:</strong> Rhythmic collision occurs when a steady pulse remains audible while accents or layered patterns emphasize different points against it.</p>
<p class="lens-builds"><strong>How it functions:</strong> By separating the underlying beat from the heard emphasis, a composer can create tension, momentum, and changing degrees of rhythmic pressure.</p>
<p class="lens-q"><em>What remains steady, and what pulls against it?</em></p>
<p class="lens-cue">Keep the steady, evenly spaced pulse while a second pattern places its emphasis across the beat. The friction comes from the two layers remaining perceptible at once.</p>
<div class="realmusic viz-embed">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Isolate the element</span><span class="rm-work">Pulse in balance · pulse under pressure</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">The first example establishes a steady pulse and repeating ground. The second preserves that pulse while an upper figure groups the same span in long · long · short arcs. Its accents pull across the expected points of emphasis until the upper pattern leaves and the underlying pulse remains.</p>
