<div><span class="lens-idx">04 · Color</span><h2 class="lens-title">Timbre as musical material</h2></div>
</div>
<p class="lens-idea"><strong>The musical idea:</strong> Timbre, or tone color, is the quality that lets us distinguish one instrument or kind of sound from another even when pitch and loudness are similar.</p>
<p class="lens-builds"><strong>How it functions:</strong> A composer can use changes of instrumental identity, register, articulation, and blend to articulate a line, transform an idea, or mark sections of musical form.</p>
<p class="lens-q"><em>Does the music move because the notes change, the sound changes, or both?</em></p>
<p class="lens-cue">First follow the same short musical cell through three instrumental colors. Then hear contrasting sound-worlds expand into successive sections.</p>
<div class="realmusic viz-embed">
<div class="realmusic-head"><span class="rm-tag">Isolate the element</span><span class="rm-work">One cell, three colors · color becomes structure</span></div>
<p class="rm-intro">The first example holds a four-note cell constant while three instruments give it different identities. This separates what is played from the sound that carries it. The second enlarges the principle: three contrasting sound fields articulate three successive formal sections. Color first transforms a cell, then helps organize a span of music.</p>
